*How it started*

## Our story

Rhythm Culture started with a shared passion. Co-founders Bec and Nathaniel Combs both fell in love with West African drum and dance in Melbourne, learning from inspiring teachers in a close-knit music community.

Before long, that passion took them to the source. Nathaniel travelled to [Conakry, Guinea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conakry) in 2004 to play with leading ensembles, and Bec later studied dance there too. Every trip deepened their respect for the rhythms, stories and people behind the music.

In 2012, they brought it all to the Sunshine Coast and founded Rhythm Culture. Their goal was simple: to share the joy of West African rhythm with their new community.

Today, that small start has grown into incursions, workshops and performances right across Australia. In fact, Drum Sing Dance is now one of the most popular kids’ incursions on the coast. Meanwhile, our Tuesday night classes in Palmwoods have become a weekly celebration of live music and community.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
![Rhythm Culture teachers and students drumming together in a circle](/media/2017/12/teachers_circle_about-us-page.jpg)
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% section tone="grey" %}
*Our approach*

## How we share West African music

Everything we teach is based on traditional West African rhythms. So whether it’s a kindy session or a corporate workshop, our approach stays the same.

{% columns layout="equal" align="top" %}
{% column %}
### Traditional rhythms

We keep every rhythm, ensemble and dance as close to its original structure as possible.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Story and song

We also include story and song in every lesson, so people understand what each rhythm means and why it’s played.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Always learning

Our teachers keep studying and training in West African culture. As a result, what we share stays authentic.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}
{% /section %}

*The people behind the rhythm*

## Meet our co-founders

{% columns layout="equal" align="top" %}
{% column %}
![Bec Combs, co-founder and dance teacher at Rhythm Culture](/media/2017/11/Bec-Combs-Full-Bio.jpg)

*Co-founder & dance teacher*

### Bec Combs

Bec began African dance in Melbourne, first learning Soukous and then Senegalese and Guinean styles. Since co-founding Rhythm Culture in 2012, she has studied dance in California and in Guinea, West Africa, where she trained with members of the renowned Merveille Ballet. Bec also brings a flair for business that keeps Rhythm Culture running smoothly.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
![Nathaniel Combs, co-founder and drummer at Rhythm Culture](/media/2017/11/Nathaniel-Combs_Full-Bio.jpg)

*Co-founder, drummer & drum maker*

### Nathaniel Combs

Nathaniel started playing percussion at seven years old in New Zealand. In 2004, he travelled to Conakry, Guinea, where he played with leading ensembles. Today, he creates all our drumming programs, leads the Muso Foli Kan Drum and Dance Ensemble, and also makes and repairs the djembes and dunduns in our shop.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% section tone="dark" %}
*Our team*

## Our artists around Australia

Behind every incursion and workshop is a team of skilled artists. They’re based in Queensland, New South Wales, Victoria, South Australia and Western Australia. That way, we can bring the same experience to schools and workplaces nationwide.

Every one of our artists has studied West African music and dance. So they share real stories and first-hand knowledge, not just the rhythms.
{% /section %}

